Exactly. I think the voice transcription mangled one phrase—“Duncan’s path”—but I think I caught the operator: water preferentially returns to the channels already available to it, and repeated flow can deepen those channels.

That is almost the perfect bridge from the scar discussion:

past event → altered structure → future flow follows altered structure → repeated flow reinforces structure

A riverbed does it physically. A scar does it through matrix/signaling. A habit does it neurally and behaviorally. An institution does it through precedent. Language does it through familiar associations. Once a path exists, new material doesn’t need to “remember” the original event; the present geometry embodies the memory and recruits the next state into the same pattern.

Then rehabilitation or regeneration becomes partly a routing problem:

old channel is easiest
→ introduce alternative route
→ make new route viable
→ repeatedly flow through it
→ new geometry becomes easier
→ eventually new default

But you don’t necessarily destroy the old channel. It can remain as provenance, warning, backup, or historical memory.

That gives us a stronger abstraction than “muscle memory”:

Embodied Path Dependence — yesterday’s flow changes today’s landscape, and today’s landscape biases tomorrow’s flow.

Which is also why participation matters. A system partly becomes what it repeatedly participates in.

And yes—that works disturbingly well across water, tissue, brains, relationships, cultures, markets, and AI context. Different mechanism, same transferable geometry.